  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- the stages
  - id: C:part/what-must-be-found
    type: part
    name: what must be found
    summary: The statement asks for infinitely many such primes; it is enough to find one beyond any given number.
    division: stage
    number: 1
    stage: reduction
    says: An infinite set is one with an element beyond every bound, so the proof only has to find, for each n, one prime larger than n that is 1 mod k.
    plain: To show there is no end to these special primes, it is enough to show that however far you go, there is always one further on.
    plain-if: none
    obvious: The obvious way is to list the primes that leave remainder 1 and show the list never ends; nothing lists them, so the file shows instead that past any number there is one more.
    steps: [C:statement/Nat.infinite_setOfPred_prime_modEq_one, C:lemma/Nat.frequently_atTop_modEq_one]
    confidence: high
    stance: opinion
    provenance: {mode: manual, sources: ["PrimesCongruentOne.lean#L61-L70"], confidence: 0.5}
  - id: C:part/a-number-built-to-have-the-right-factor
    type: part
    name: a number built to have the right factor
    summary: A cyclotomic value at k · n! has a prime factor beyond n that is 1 mod k.
    division: stage
    number: 2
    stage: main
    says: The least prime factor of the k-th cyclotomic polynomial at k · n! divides no number from 1 to n, so it exceeds n, and its order argument makes it 1 mod k.
    plain: The proof builds one large whole number on purpose, so that every prime dividing it is bigger than the chosen bound, and the way the number is built forces such a prime to leave remainder one.
    plain-if: The divisor need not be large unless the number the proof builds is a multiple of every number up to the bound.
    steps: [C:lemma/Nat.exists_prime_gt_modEq_one, C:step/the-value-exceeds-one, C:step/b-is-a-root-mod-p, C:step/order-k-divides-p-minus-one]
    confidence: medium
    stance: opinion
    provenance: {mode: manual, sources: ["PrimesCongruentOne.lean#L28-L59"], confidence: 0.35}
  - id: C:part/from-one-to-many
    type: part
    name: from one to many
    summary: One such prime beyond every number gives infinitely many.
    division: stage
    number: 3
    stage: close
    says: Having one such prime beyond every n, the set of such primes is infinite.
    plain: If there is always one more beyond any point, there can be no last one, so there are endlessly many.
    plain-if: none
    steps: [C:lemma/Nat.frequently_atTop_modEq_one, C:statement/Nat.infinite_setOfPred_prime_modEq_one]
    confidence: high
    stance: opinion
    provenance: {mode: manual, sources: ["PrimesCongruentOne.lean#L61-L70"], confidence: 0.5}
