  - id: P:case/k-three-n-two
    type: case
    name: k = 3, n = 2
    summary: The yield worked by hand for k = 3 and n = 2.
    of: P:yield/what-the-proof-delivers
    from: worked
    stands: inside
    given: k = 3 and n = 2. The point is 3 · 2! = 6. The third cyclotomic polynomial is x² + x + 1.
    expect: At 6 it gives 43. Its least prime factor is 43, which is larger than 2 and equals 3 · 14 + 1.
    confidence: high
    stance: opinion
    provenance: {mode: manual, sources: ["the design's worked example"], confidence: 0.5}
  - id: P:reading/the-polynomial-does-the-work
    type: reading
    name: the polynomial does the work
    summary: Every prime factor of a cyclotomic value, apart from those dividing k, is 1 mod k; the proof only has to make one large.
    of: C:part/a-number-built-to-have-the-right-factor
    whose: {by: model}
    says: The cyclotomic polynomial is chosen because its prime factors not dividing the point are 1 mod k; the factorial in the point pushes them past n.
    grounds: [{of: C:step/b-is-a-root-mod-p, lines: "43-50"}, {of: C:step/order-k-divides-p-minus-one, lines: "53-59"}]
